  <section id="philosophy">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="eyebrow">How I teach</span><h2>My Teaching Philosophy</h2></div>
    <div class="about">
      <div class="about-copy">
        <p>My philosophy of teaching is to create a supportive environment that allows for guided exploration. I believe that the most significant learning happens when tasks are broken down into meaningful, achievable steps that build a student's confidence. My goal is to place learners in structured situations where they can practice skills in a way that feels safe and manageable: using visual schedules to master transitions, practicing social cues in a controlled small group setting, or following a step by step prompt hierarchy to complete a daily living task. For genuine progress to occur, the learner must have a clear, consistent path from total support to independent success.</p>
        <p>For me, the best way to accomplish these goals is through one on one or small group instruction. This focused setting is key: it allows me to tailor the pace to the student's specific needs and provides the emotional security necessary for them to take risks. Teaching in a special education setting requires a delicate balance between providing enough support to prevent frustration and enough space to allow for growth. The environment must be structured enough so that the student knows exactly what is expected of them, but flexible enough so that they feel empowered to try new things as they move through each step of their learning process.</p>
        <p>I have applied this philosophy during my time as a paraprofessional. For students struggling with communication, I facilitate growth by introducing basic prompts and gradually fading them as the student gains autonomy. I support social emotional development by guiding students through behavioral scripts and role playing, ensuring they meet their goals while still having the space to express their own feelings. New students are introduced to a predictable routine so they can feel secure in their surroundings. In a step program setting, most of the learning happens through repetition and reinforcement. This one on one approach provides a controlled environment to develop skills in self regulation, task completion, and social interaction. Students on an individualized track learn to navigate their day with the help of a support team, learning not just the skill itself, but how to use their tools to overcome obstacles.</p>
        <p>In a high support classroom, a ‘safe environment’ means a space where the student feels emotionally secure and physically safe to fail and try again. Maintaining a predictable, low stress atmosphere is essential to prevent overwhelm. Teaching in this environment is challenging, but using a graduated responsibility approach helps students move toward independence. This requires a constant assessment of where the student is today compared to yesterday, and a willingness to adjust the "step" if the current goal is too high or too low. Clear, consistent feedback and a positive reinforcement system are vital to keeping the student motivated.</p>
        <p>Ultimately, the goal is to move from a teacher led environment to one where the student takes ownership of their progress. By using visual checklists and success folders, I help students see how far they have come. This fosters a sense of pride and self advocacy, instilling habits of persistence that will serve them long after they leave my classroom.</p>
